From 57bdb9bb43dffe9ad4fb5c02c78ee5996db0d93c Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: AlexProgrammerDE <40795980+AlexProgrammerDE@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 6 Oct 2026 04:35:53 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] feat(movement): send completed local physics to ViaBedrock

Send the local player's position, predicted motion and swimming
state after sendChanges and before Java CLIENT_TICK_END. Use the
negotiated, versioned core channel on direct connections and ViaProxy.
A stationary player still sends a fresh sample, so fluid gravity and pose
transitions do not depend on Java's sparse position updates.

Remove the Entity-wide swimming hook, which could turn another entity's
pose change into local auth input and required an in-process core tracker.
The core now validates and consumes local frames and emits swimming edges.
This integrates the existing Bedrock fluid physics with auth-input packet
construction without changing server corrections or vehicle prediction.

Native 1.26.51.1 protocol 2193 captures report -0.022315647 fluid motion
where the old core's air approximation reports -0.0996117 at the same
water position. Java 26.3 bytecode confirms the sampled method runs after
local physics and standard movement, before the tick-end packet.

Native sprint swimming starts on the first movement frame. Java updates
swimming before aiStep computes sprinting, delaying its transition by one
frame. Refresh the local swimming state after sprint changes and before
fluid travel on Bedrock connections. Keep ordinary Java timing intact.

Run the local swimming update after sprint decisions and before fluid travel.
Native 1.26.51.1 wall and open-water captures stop swimming on forward release
while retaining sprint drag until the next frame. Preserve that physics
phase directly instead of delaying protocol events independently. Include
completed collision axes so stationary frames cannot retain stale flags.

Keep the fluid inset symmetric for short swimming boxes. Java normalizes
inverted AABBs, which moved the water boundary below the box center and
delayed native surface exits. Avoid the stale visual crawling slowdown
on that exit frame and retain sprinting in shallow water. Native 1.26.51.1
protocol 2193 surface captures establish these transitions.

Inspect the matching native SwimTriggerSystem and PlayerBoundingBoxStateUpdateSystem
to reproduce upward swimming re-entry. Native checks the breathing-point
material and the block above the single-precision collision-box center when
look Y is at least 0.15. Gate the local transition with those probes instead
of fitting an eye height or frame delay to the trajectory. Preserve the
previous swimming state for the verified sprint-release phase.

Stop upward swimming when the native breathing probe reaches air and
standing is unobstructed. Reproduce the trigger's acos of the squared
horizontal direction components instead of using the body-water predicate.
The first revised strict-BDS run exposed that difference on the second
surface exit after re-entry. The completed direct comparison now matches
the native pose and sprint events at all three reference angles without
corrections during the controlled swimming cases.

Use the matching native SwimControlSystem's breathing-material condition
for non-jumping, non-flying upward swimming. Clear vertical motion whenever
the breathing point is outside liquid, rather than applying a fitted
look-Y cutoff of 0.55. Preserve swimming when the standing-space probe
is blocked, as the native swimming trigger does.

Ceiling re-entry requires the native water-contact input bypass described
below. The revised strict-BDS cases pass without corrections on both
routes. Metadata tick handling and history resimulation remain separate
incomplete requirements. Native open-water captures cover the separate
unobstructed cases.

Sample ground-jump impulses from the local jumpFromGround method in the
same completed physics frame. Reset the observation before aiStep and
carry it over the negotiated revision-five prediction channel. Core
constructs StartJumping from that observation on direct and ViaProxy
connections. Liquid-rise input does not imply a ground-jump impulse.

Native 1.26.51.1 protocol 2193 pool-floor captures omit StartJumping on
held water rises while preserving Jumping, JumpDown and WantUp. Ground
jumps include StartJumping. Observe the physical action so blocked jumps,
automatic jumps and stationary frames do not depend on a fitted velocity
threshold or a held-key heuristic.

Native 1.26.51.1 CurrentSwimAmountSystem updates the single-precision
swimming blend by 0.1 toward zero or one. MobJumpSystem suppresses water
rises while this blend lies strictly between those bounds, including
after swimming stops. Its callback adapter supplies WasInWater, not
MobIsJumping, for the fluid gate; jumping is already required by the view.

The old ten-tick counter resets on swimming release and permits immediate
water rises during the exit transition. Strict BDS corrects that path back
to the pool floor. Use the completed blend, preserve its decay after
release, and retain the water and jumping gates. Adjust Java's 0.09 blend
step to the native 0.1 on Bedrock connections. Leave non-water jumps
outside this suppression. Broader native phase and fluid cases remain
separate verification requirements.

Reproduce MobJumpSystem's separate swimming-with-head-out-of-water gate.
The matching ActorHeadInWater update uses the breathing query at zero
interpolation and compares it with the native liquid-depth surface.
Use single-precision division, offset and block-top subtraction, preserving
source and falling water at the full block top. Java's source-water height
would cap the rise before the native head boundary.

Keep the existing unmounted breathing-point integration and water/jump
scope. Strict BDS accepts the held-jump surface cap without correcting the
previous repeated rises. Player-specific breathing offsets, mounted
geometry, native combined-input captures and other fluid states remain
separate verification requirements.

Gate SwimControlSystem on the native WasInWater component. An airborne
swimming pose does not run its vertical controller or head-out motion
cap. Preserve the previous motion before ordinary air gravity instead
of replacing it with zero on the body-water exit frame.

Matching 1.26.51.1 executable inspection establishes the required view
component. Strict-BDS direct and ViaProxy cases now retain the accepted
first air-frame motion at both 35 and 45 degrees. Saved unobstructed
native surface frames and the held swimming jump remain unchanged.
The water-contact input bypass below also removes the ceiling re-entry
corrections on both routes. Server actor metadata still requires tick-aware
history reconciliation for broader prediction parity.

Retain the core crawling pose when Java recalculates the local player pose each tick. Strict BDS 1.26.51.1 clears SWIMMING while setting CRAWLING under a ceiling and retains its 0.6-block body. Use the existing native actor state channel for both direct and ViaProxy connections. Do not change the swimming flag or overwrite sleeping, gliding or spin attack poses. Core supplies the pose for remote players, whose Java updatePlayerPose method is empty.

Evaluate core's native posture rule after local input scaling and before
travel. Query block collisions with the pinned native standing height
1.8F, crouching height 1.49F, crawling height 0.6F and 0.01F inset. Native
SneakTriggerIntentSystem switches swimming into crawling when body water
ends under a ceiling, then restores swimming on water entry. Keep the
local crawling state independent from delayed actor snapshot arrival.

Send the three completed posture flags on core's revision-five channel.
Core emits crawling/swimming edges and distinguishes physical sneak from
forced crouching. Preserve input scaling before this frame's posture
change and native block-space probes during Java pose selection. Keep
sleeping, gliding and spin attack outside the crawling pose override.

This follows private inspection of the pinned 1.26.51.1 executable's
SneakTriggerIntentSystem, PlayerBoundingBoxStateUpdateSystem and MoveInput
function. Metadata history rewind, native breathing offsets, other input
controllers and broader posture conditions still require verification.

Use core's prior-posture input scaling decision in local physics. Matching
1.26.51.1 calculator 0x1404495e0 and adapter 0x14669d7d0 bypass sneak/crawl
slowdown during flight, previous SWIMMING, or WasInWaterFlagComponent.
Sample water contact before input and carry it in the revision-5 frame so
core emits the same move vector, including initial crawl-to-water input.

Use core's native equipped Swift Sneak scalar for local input, preserving
separate float multiply/add arithmetic and the cap at one. Native
SneakingSystem 0x14b192c50 and callback 0x14b193600 establish the rule;
equipment update 0x1432760e0 selects leggings and enchantment 37. Java
uses a double attribute with different rounding, while core previously
sent fixed 0.3 input despite translated enchanted leggings. Keep the
water/swimming/flight bypass and ordinary Java attribute behavior intact.

The native scalar producer grants the enchantment bonus only when the
prior SNEAKING or CRAWLING flag is set. The initial physical sneak press
therefore uses 0.3 before the posture transition. Apply the same previous
posture in core and local physics. Keep normalized diagonal input instead
of Java's square-speed expansion, which strict BDS rejects while sneaking.
The native input calculator normalizes before applying the sneak scalar.

Equipment update 0x1432760e0 creates SwiftSneakEnchantComponent only when
absent and removes it for nonpositive enchantment levels. Positive-to-
positive replacement retains the original component level. Share this
lifecycle in SwiftSneakState and advance equipment state even while idle.
Strict BDS produces the same retained-level behavior; do not fit physics
to a newly read level that the pinned native component has not adopted.

Native parser 0x14519b430 reads the low byte of a short enchantment ID,
defaults incorrectly typed IDs/levels to zero, and preserves entry order.
Lookup 0x1432602c0 takes the first matching entry. Share that level lookup
with valid Java enchantment translation so duplicate entries cannot give
local physics a different Swift Sneak level from the core input vector.

Use core's retained SneakingInputState for the scalar phase. Native
registration 0x14681da00 orders input before the scalar producer and applies
sneak actions later. Consume the retained component, produce its successor
from prior posture, then apply the new posture, including idle frames.
This reproduces startup and release without an elapsed-tick workaround.

Apply the core-translated native USE_EFFECTS multiplier after posture
input, matching ItemUseSlowdownApplySystem at 0x14c60f9a0 in 1.26.51.1.
Suppress Java's earlier use multiplier only on Bedrock connections.
Snapshot the applied scale for the completed frame and transport it on
revision 6, including ViaProxy. Clear it before each frame, and bypass
item-use slowdown for passengers. Consuming or replacing the selected
item later cannot reconstruct a different factor for that frame.

Bound local Bedrock item-use duration at zero. Matching native duration
callback 0x14c6109b0 decrements positive ticks only and clamps at zero.
Java 26.3 updateUsingItem otherwise continues below zero while awaiting
server completion. Keep authoritative inventory updates and Java's server
acknowledgment path, and limit this change to the local Bedrock player.

Native Level+0x238 supplies completion's virtual+0x9e8 side gate.
Client factory 0x14086eef0 sets LevelArguments+0x120 to one; base Level
constructor 0x14114e0c0 copies that flag, and ClientLevel 0x1412efe30
retains it. Native local completion therefore skips the server inventory
transaction and CompletedUsingItem sender. Its ItemCoordinator event and
the separate ActorUseItem acknowledgment still have untraced consumers.
A local completion prototype exposed delayed updates interrupting the
next use cycle in strict BDS. Retain only the established bounded counter
while completion, event consumers and correction history remain open.

Keep local item-use transitions independent of translated actor flags. In
Bedrock 1.26.51.1, Actor::startUsingItem and stopUsingItem maintain the local
active-use field separately from ActorDataFlagComponent. Flag assignment
only updates kinetic-damage state through the inspected using-item callback.
Java 26.3 LocalPlayer::onSyncedDataUpdated otherwise starts or stops that
local cycle from metadata, which lets delayed updates interrupt repeated use.
Guard those two metadata transitions for local Bedrock players. Preserve
metadata itself, remote players, ordinary Java sessions, selected-item
identity checks, explicit release, and inventory/completion handling.

Use core GlideMovement for completed local glide travel on direct and
ViaProxy Bedrock sessions. Native callback 0x146667b10 in build 1.26.51.1
uses float arithmetic, reconstructed view angles, native trigonometry,
and Slow Falling gravity even during ascent. Preserve its fall-distance
reset to one when entering velocity Y exceeds -0.5. Keep Java movement,
collision dispatch and completed-frame transport around the shared kernel.

The core calculation matches 4,366 exact native execution cases with
supplied CRT remainder, sine table, status slots and no rocket boost.
Java-only sessions and remote entities retain their existing travel.
Rocket duration, native input phases, collision solving, correction replay
and live direct/ViaProxy flight comparisons remain open requirements.

Consume the core movement-effect payload and advance an explicit frame
identity with each completed local physics sample. Apply confirmed rocket
boosts in shared glide travel using their original frame binding; late
packets expire against that frame, never a new receipt-time countdown.
Use prediction revision 7 consistently with core on direct and ViaProxy.
Reject older confirmations, clear effects on disconnect and remove actor
state when its translated entity disappears.

Suppress Java's separate rocket impulse for the local Bedrock player so
an attached Java firework cannot double the native boost or substitute its
random lifespan. Other clients and remote entities retain Java behavior.
Core native arithmetic matches 8,732 controlled execution cases. Snapshot
phase scheduling, speculative item prediction, correction replay, actual
native flight trajectories and both live routes remain unverified.

Receive core underwater movement snapshots on direct and ViaProxy sessions
and clear them on disconnect or actor removal. Use the shared native
acceleration and damping only for a swimming local player with a confirmed
dolphin boost and a known authoritative underwater attribute. Preserve zero
and do not substitute an inferred default speed for missing server state.

Matching 1.26.51.1 callbacks 0x148b99e40, 0x14207c030 and 0x142dee630 establish
boost admission, full Depth Strider scaling, sprint drag and unmodified
water drag during boosts. The core kernels match all 6,992 speed/damping
native cases exactly. The client supplies equipped enchantment and water
trait observations; core owns calculation, authoritative state and wire
format. Phase timing, trait mapping, speculative admission, correction
replay and actual boosted trajectories on both routes remain unverified.

Use the shared WaterMovement calculator for ordinary water travel as well
as confirmed dolphin boosts. Keep the authoritative underwater attribute
gate and observe effective walking speed, equipped Depth Strider, ground,
sprint and water drag at the existing client travel boundaries. Airborne
ordinary travel uses half enchantment efficiency; confirmed boosted travel
keeps full efficiency. Preserve native multiplication/division order and
float damping instead of Java's interpolation order and double motion.

Core production calculations match all 19,456 bounded native executions.
Keep the calculator and its rules in core and retain no dolphin-only shim.
The existing completed-frame transport handles both connection routes.
Native phase timing, walking-speed/trait mapping, input rotation, collision,
fluid gravity, currents and correction history remain unverified or partial.

Retain local movement attributes and confirmed effects while the client
player temporarily leaves the world entity index during death or respawn.
Connection teardown still clears them, and unloaded remote actors are
pruned. Strict BDS repeats corrections after respawn when underwater speed
is lost; a runtime observation confirms 0.02 becomes absent while equipped
Depth Strider remains level three. Preserve connection-owned local state
instead of falling back to Java water physics until another attribute update.

Use the core FluidCurrent calculator for observed liquid flows. Replace
the former double normalization adjustments with native float accumulation,
normalization threshold and impulse addition. Apply native .014F water and
.0035F lava strengths independently from Java fast-lava policy. Empty or
fully cancelled flows retain entering motion without an unnecessary cast.
Reset the accumulator on every observation frame and retain Java behavior
outside Bedrock connections. Per-cell flow generation, cell enumeration,
mixed-material selection and loaded-world scheduling remain separate gaps.

Use the shared native separated-axis contact rule for Bedrock
voxel-shape collision queries. Convert each actual shape box to
float32 without replacing separate boxes with an envelope. A
rounded teleport can place Java bounds just inside a wall face,
where Java grid traversal stops clipping current-driven motion.
Native contact distances within 0.000001F still block that face.
Preserve unblocked double displacement and the ordinary Java path.
Native actor overlap recovery, shape ordering, axis sequencing,
and stepping remain incomplete and are not claimed by this change.

Apply core-provided grounded state after the matching Java position
correction. Standard player-position packets omit this state, while a
zero-relative entity teleport starts local interpolation in Java 26.3.
Use core's player and teleport identity pairing without changing
position, velocity, acknowledgments, or other entities. Clear pending
state across connection changes and mismatched position packets.

Apply the shared core geyser calculation at the start of local travel
with confirmed movement effects, float body coordinates and current
world collision/fluid observations. Preserve the original velocity when
no boost applies and exclude the Flying ability. Remove the local player
from Java's geyser launch ticker to prevent a second impulse and a Java
fall-distance reset. Core owns the scan, profiles, gates and rounding;
the client supplies world observations on direct and ViaProxy routes.

The pinned native callback and four helpers match 6,780 cases. Client
world adapters distinguish sulfur states, source/flowing/falling fluids
and waterlogged collision shapes. Full native phase ordering, speculative
geyser admission, live trajectories and history replay remain required.

Observe sulfur block entity ticks before Java's launch-range checks and
feed the local actor bounds into core native geyser admission. Refresh
the effect timeline while the actor overlaps the source range, then
apply lift during travel. Java's launch impulse and fall-distance reset
remain excluded for the local Bedrock actor. Native command-history
replay and full-world trajectories remain separate verification gates.

Replace Java flight input and the hard-zero idle shortcut with the shared
core native flight calculation. Preserve normal filtered jump and sneak
requests, capture post-collision motion before Java gravity and friction,
and defer Java's motion cutoff to the native friction phase. Keep the
ordinary Java path and camera, passenger, connection and ability gates.

Matching Bedrock 1.26.51.1 protocol 2193 controller, drag and friction
routines match 18,000 control cases and 3,456 friction cases bit for bit.
The independent authoritative flight speed, raw inherited mode and native
modifier snapshot travels through the negotiated core channel on both
routes. Java has no independent slow ascent or descent controls, so do not
invent bindings. Native trajectories, strict BDS and Boar remain separate
verification requirements.

Defer Java's four motion-cutoff comparisons with composable boolean
expressions. The initial constant hook collided with pinned VFP's
modifyVelocityZero hook at actual client startup. Preserve that threshold
hook and the computed magnitudes; suppress comparisons only for active
native flight. Include Java's separate player horizontal-squared cutoff.
Keep explicit four-match requirements and ordinary Java behavior.

Preserve the actor swimming flag during negotiated native flight instead of
Player.isSwimming's Java flying exclusion. Apply the core float SwimControl
1.3F interpolation before travel; suppress the Java swimming interpolation.
Clear only the effective physical jump read while flying, preserving normal
ascent/descent keys and the raw jump requests sent to Bedrock.

Pinned 1.26.51.1/protocol 2193 startup registration and original category
executor establish VerticalFlySpeedControl -> JumpInput -> SwimControl.
Original callbacks and a 12-step retained-velocity sequence confirm flying
jump removal, normal/simultaneous requests and swimming/material transitions.
Use composable return/expression hooks with exact supported target counts.
The versioned flight context, local camera actor and nonpassenger gates keep
ordinary Java and unnegotiated sessions on their existing behavior.

Targeted test-only Mockito transport/context fixtures cover positive swimming
retention, negative context loss, flight stop, passenger/camera exclusions,
ordinary Java and actual native swimming assignment without raw jump mutation.
Native look-table entries were supplied in callback tests. Runtime Java look
and material producers, full collision and native/strict BDS/direct/ViaProxy
trajectories remain verification boundaries. Nonflying local swimming still
uses Java interpolation pending its complete jump and input binding proof.

Preserve the SwimControl pitch producer through wrapped current-minus-previous
float degrees, negative float radians and the shared Windows sine lookup.
The actual 1.26.51.1 callback 1490c0a00 with the separately verified official
Windows table matches every look and motion bit across 26,660 supplied cases.
Java 26.3 Mth differs at index and table rounding boundaries. Keep this helper
in core and its client binding limited to negotiated local swimming flight.
Rotation component construction, scheduler and breathing material producers
remain separate native boundaries. These checks do not prove live trajectories.
